  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nFor Thursday, some chance PoPs stick around in the \nnorthern combined Panhandles as wrap around moisture circles the \nupper level pressure system as it comes across. The southern half of \nthe combined Panhandles should stay mostly dry slotted, depending on \nthe track of the low. However, some very light precip still may fall \nbriefly Thursday afternoon as far south as the I-40 corridor. The \nheavy rain should be over by this time and any flooding will likely \nhave already occurred, with the exception of potential river/creek \nflooding down stream from Tuesday into Wednesday's rain. The main \nstory Thursday will be the arrival of an accompanied cold front \nthanks to the upper level trough. This front is progged to bring in \nsome breezy and gusty northerly winds during the day. Highest winds \nthat day are looking to be out west along the NM state line where \ngust could be as high as 50 mph. NBM gives about a 20 percent chance \nfor gusts exceeding 50 mph for the Kenton, OK to Texline, TX area at \nthis time. Slight chance PoPs Thursday night taper off going into \nFriday. \n\nPost frontal afternoon highs on Friday may very well be in the 60s \nfor much of the area. But the combined Panhandles should see \nafternoon highs rebound into the 70s to maybe 80 Saturday into \nMonday.",
